.TH NMEA 2
.SH NAME
nmea \- NMEA 0183 sentences as GPS fixes and traffic
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "nmea.m";
nmea := load Nmea Nmea->PATH;
Fix, Parser, Sat, Target: import nmea;

init:      fn();
fields:    fn(s: string): (array of string, string);
timeof:    fn(s: string): string;
sentences: fn(f: ref Fix): string;

Parser.new:        fn(): ref Parser;
p.sentence:        fn(s: string): (list of ref Fix, string);
p.satellites:      fn(): string;
p.forget:          fn();
p.target:          fn(t: ref Target);
p.traffic:         fn(now: int): string;
p.last:            ref Fix;

Target.new:        fn(kind, id: string): ref Target;
t.text:            fn(now: int): string;

f.time:            fn(): string;
f.text:            fn(): string;
f.position:        fn(): string;
f.fixmode:         fn(): int;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Nmea
reads the sentences a GPS receiver sends and assembles them into fixes.
.B Init
must be called first.
.PP
.B Fields
checks one sentence (a trailing newline and carriage return are
removed) and splits it at its commas, the address first:
.B $GPRMC,1,2*hh
gives
.BR "GPRMC 1 2" .
A sentence must begin with
.B $
(or
.BR ! ,
an encapsulated one: AIS);
its checksum, if it has one, must be right.
The error is
.BR "not a sentence" ,
.BR "checksum mismatch" ,
.BR "bad checksum field" ,
.BR "bad character" ,
.B "bad address field"
or
.BR "sentence too long" .
.PP
.B Timeof
gives the time a sentence carries, as
.IR hh : mm : ss ,
with a fraction if the receiver gives one, or nil.
.PP
A
.B Parser
takes a receiver's sentences in order.
.B Sentence
gives the fixes the sentence completed, oldest first, usually none or
one, or the error
.B fields
found.
RMC, GGA, GLL, GSA, GSV, GST, VTG and ZDA are read, from any
constellation's talker
.RB ( GP ,
.BR GL ,
.BR GA ,
.BR GB ,
.BR BD ,
.BR GQ ,
.BR GI ,
.BR GN ),
or a ship's instruments
.RB ( II ,
.BR IN ,
.BR EC ).
So are what aviation and marine receivers add: the magnetic variation
(RMC's), a compass heading (HDT; HDG with its deviation and variation;
HDM once the variation is known), Garmin's pressure altitude and error
estimates (PGRMZ in feet or metres; PGRME, unless GST is sent), AIS
(VDM, messages 1, 2, 3, 5, 18, 19 and 24, a message of several
sentences put together; VDO, a ship's own, is passed over) and FLARM's
traffic (PFLAA, placed from the fix).
Other sentences are taken and passed over.
A receiver sends a burst of sentences for each time it computes a
position, which ones and in what order varying by receiver.
A fix is complete when every kind of position sentence the receiver
has been seen to send (RMC, GGA, GLL) has arrived for one time, or a
sentence for a new time arrives first.
A fix with no position is given only when the last one had one, or
was the first.
.PP
.B P.last
is the last complete fix; sentences after it for the same time (a
GSA) are added to it.
.B Satellites
gives one line for each satellite in view: its system, PRN, elevation,
azimuth, signal to noise ratio, and 1 if it is in the fix, 0 if not,
with
.B -
for a value not known.
.B Forget
is for a new receiver: what the old one sent is forgotten, its last
fix kept.
.PP
A
.B Fix
holds what the receiver gave for one time.
Each value has a flag or a sentinel for unknown: the
.B has
flags, -1 for
.B sats
and
.BR mode ,
a negative value for a dilution or an error.
Latitude and longitude are degrees, north and east positive;
.B altitude
is metres above mean sea level;
.B speed
metres a second;
.B course
degrees true;
.B err
and
.B verr
metres, one sigma, from GST.
.B Time
is RFC 3339 if the date is known (from RMC or ZDA), nil if not.
.B Text
is the fix as one line of attr=value fields, those known only; see
.IR gpsfs (4).
.B Position
is
.RI ` "lat lon" '
to six places, or nil when there is no fix.
.B Fixmode
is
.BR Mnone ,
.B M2d
or
.BR M3d ,
the receiver's if it gave one.
.B Magvar
is degrees, east positive;
.B heading
degrees true;
.B baroalt
metres.
.PP
A
.B Target
is another craft:
.B kind
.BR ais ,
.B adsb
or
.BR flarm ,
.B id
its MMSI or address, and what is known of its position, altitude,
speed, course, heading, climb, name, call sign and category, each with
a flag.
.B Seen
is
.IR sys->millisec ()
when it was last heard.
.B P.target
merges a target into what the parser knows of it (AIS sends a ship's
name and its position in different messages).
.B P.traffic
gives one line a target heard in the ten minutes before
.IR now ,
nearest first when there is a fix, as
.B T.text
writes it; older ones are forgotten.
.PP
.B Sentences
goes the other way: a fix written as the sentences a receiver would
send for it, an RMC (with the variation), a GST if there are error
estimates, a GGA if there is a position, an HDT if there is a heading
and a PGRMZ if there is a pressure altitude, each ending CR LF.
Read back by a
.BR Parser ,
they give the fix again.
.IR Gpsfs (4)
uses it for receivers that do not speak NMEA
.RI ( gdl90 (2),
.IR garminav (2)).
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/nmea.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR gdl90 (2),
.IR garminav (2),
.IR gpsfs (4)
